<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jebel Barkal</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gebel Barkal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jebel Barkal is a flat-topped sandstone mountain near Karima in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, held sacred as a home of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>. Egyptian kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> built a temple at its foot, and the Kushite kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a> enlarged it as the religious centre of their state; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piye">Piye</a> set up his victory stela there. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jebel-barkal.jpg" alt="Jebel Barkal" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LassiHU · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gebel_Barkal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jebel Barkal is a flat-topped mesa 104 metres tall, standing 400 km north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a> beside Karima in Northern State, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>. It acquired religious significance for both ancient Kush and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> who occupied it. The earliest occupation is known so far only from scattered potsherds of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a> culture, whose people seem to have venerated the mesa in an animistic fashion and to have passed the tradition on to the New Kingdom Egyptians and later to the Napatans and Meroites.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Around 1450 BCE the pharaoh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a> conquered Jebel Barkal and built a fortified settlement there as the southern limit of the Egyptian empire. The city and region became known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a>, and Egyptian occupation lasted through most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> built a complex of temples centred on a temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> of Napata, a local, ram-headed form of the god of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. Little was built in the last years of the New Kingdom or after its collapse in 1169 BCE.</p><p>Jebel Barkal was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kingdom of Kush</a> as it returned to power after 800 BCE as the Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a>. The Kushite kings who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> as the 25th Dynasty, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashta">Kashta</a>, Piankhy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taharqa">Taharqa</a>, built, renovated and expanded monuments there. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-conquest-of-egypt">Assyrian conquest of Egypt</a> in the mid-7th century BCE, the Kushites kept Jebel Barkal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a> as the main centres of their kingdom, and palaces and temples were renewed there until the early 3rd century BCE.</p><p>In 270 BCE royal burials moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a>, but Jebel Barkal remained an important city, and King Natakamani built a sequence of palaces. Pyramids of the Meroitic period were built at the site, and occupation continued in the Christian period of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> after Kush collapsed in the 4th century CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The ruins include at least 13 temples built, renovated and expanded over more than 1,500 years. European explorers began describing them in the 1820s, among them Frédéric Cailliaud, Louis Maurice Adolphe Linant de Bellefonds and Karl Richard Lepsius, whose drawings record details that have since disappeared. Scientific excavation began in 1916 with a joint expedition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harvard-university">Harvard University</a> and the Museum of Fine Arts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> under George Reisner, followed from the 1970s by a team from the University of Rome La Sapienza under Sergio Donadoni. The larger temples, such as the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>, are still regarded as sacred by the local population. In 2003 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> named the mountain and the archaeological site at its base a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jebel Barkal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A new Kushite state took shape at Napata, near the holy mountain of <b>Jebel Barkal</b>, after Egyptian control of Nubia collapsed with the end of the New Kingdom around 1070 BCE and local elites reasserted independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Some scholars believe the people of Kerma held an animist view of the world, pointing to their treatment of <b>Jebel Barkal</b> as a sacred place, a tradition later taken up by the Egyptians of the New Kingdom, who built a great temple of Amun there.</blockquote><a href="/culture/kerma-culture">Kerma culture · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Napata was a great religious centre: <b>Jebel Barkal</b>, a sandstone hill that its people saw as a rearing cobra, was declared by Egyptian priests to be the home of the god Amun, and Egyptian and Nubian gods were worshipped in Nubia for 2,500 years.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nubians">Nubians · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Pharaohs built temple towns at Soleb, Sesebi, <b>Gebel Barkal</b> and Abu Simbel.</blockquote><a href="/state/viceroyalty-of-kush">Viceroyalty of Kush</a></li></ul></section>
